With regards to your message at 11:00 PM 11-03-98 +0000, Dave Gilbert.
Where you stated:
>On Tue, 3 Nov 1998, Daniel A. Theobald wrote:
>
>> What is the difference between SCSI-2 narrow and SCSI-2 narrow
>> differential?
>> Does the controller only work for non differential?
>
>They are different physical interfaces - often with THE SAME CONNECTOR.
>
>Connecting the wrong pair together can DAMAGE the drive and or
>controller.
>
>Differential can use longer cabling.
>
>Their is now something called Ultra-2 LVD (Low-Voltage Differential) - I
>think this is supposed to interoporate with standard SCSI-2 but I'm not
>sure. Does anyone know?
>
>
>Dave

Basically the difference between SCSI Single-ended (SE) and Differential is
that a SIngle-ended interface modulates a signal voltage between a line and
ground. In the case of differential this occurs between 2 active lines.
Think of single-ended as being half of a sine-wave signal form, and
differential as being a full wave, both positive and negative.
 The main advantage is that the effective signal is much less susceptible
to the degradation effects caused by electrical resistance and capacitance.
As a result the differential interface allows much longer cables, and less
termination problems. It has a higher signal-to-noise ratio.
 The main problem with diffrential SCSI is that it uses a higher voltage
level than single-ended devices, and as Dave mentions accidentally mixing
them can cause for device damage.
Low Voltage Differential, or Ultra-2 is a newer develpment, using the same
voltage level as single-ended devices. It allows for up to 15M cables (
about 50') and is much less termination-sensitive.
 The new spec also mandates for LVD devices to be able to detect the
attachment of SE devices on the bus, and then changing their bahaviour to
SE standard if they are found. Of course if you put an SE device on an LVD
bus you lose the advantages of LVD.
Ultra2 has a higher maximum data rate than SE UltraSCSI1. 80MB per second
versus 40MB/sec on wide 16-bit buses.
  For most personal computers and workstations the differences have no
major consequence.
A fast hard disk MIGHT be capable of 25MB per second. So, the older
UltraSCSI 1 standard is more than fast enough. It is only when we use a
larger number of hard disks, and also run into the problem this causes with
cable lengths do we see a real advantage to LVD. In this case it is a very
useful.

For more, go see the real specs at the SCSI Trade Association site: 
http://www.scsita.org


