On Wed, 4 Nov 1998, David R. Kohel wrote:

> I've heard of problems with one-way laptop printer 
> ports, but is there any known obstruction for digital 
> hardware?

For what its worth, I was never successful getting my UDB to do PLIP
to my notebook either. 

> Is there any other way to connect two desktops? -- I've 
> got only one ethernet line to the hub.

This is why I like having 10b2 along with 10bT, or whatever. Why not pick
up a $20 PCI 10b2 interface for the miata, and a 10b2 PCMCIA card for
the notebook? Since you were gonna use PLIP, I expect you don't need
a whole lot of speed, and 10b2 should give you about a meg a second, which
is better than PLIP. 

I recently went this route, and I haven't regretted it. I run some form
of Unix on my desktops (Linux and *BSD) and Windows on my notebook, so
I picked up a cheap $30 10b2 PCMCIA card, for using the PCMCIA network
card under Linux, you may have to pay a few bucks more in order to find
one that works.

I just used my UDB's 10b2, and put a 3com PCI card in there to go to
my hub. 

One thing to be aware of .. most cheap 10b2 cards are NE2k clones, which
usually get set up via a DOS program (one time .. after that, they retain
the info after cold boots). So you may need a PC around to set up the
card, and then move it to the Alpha. Or.. Win NT might be able to set it
up, if you have that available. 


