>> Robin Stephenson wrote:
>> >> [...] Half-way through upgrading bash with `rpm -Uvh bash*' (can
>> >> you see this coming yet..?) I got a segv and *poof* my system
>> >> stopped working. [...]
                                [...]

Bodo> You might try adding something like "init=/bin/csh" to your boot
Bodo> options [...]

Thanks for all offers of help & suggestions: I fixed this rather
embarassing problem as follows:

* booted with `init=/bin/ash.static' added to the usual boot options
* ran fsck on all of the disks (probably not necessary, but it made me 
  feel a bit better)
* remounted the root disk read-write with `mount -t ext2 /dev/sda / -o 
  remount,rw' -- this was a sticking point for a while until it
  occurred to me to see how /etc/rc.d/rc.sysinit did it...
* with my heart in my throat ran `rpm -Uvh bash-whatever-version-it-was'
  -- which worked smoothly this time

-- 
 Robin Stephenson
