With regards to your message at 02:31 PM 11-05-98 -0800, Chris Albertson.
Where you stated:
>Sorry it if this is a FAQ but here goes...
>
>I am porting some software to Linux from Solaris.  I have it running
>on Linux/i386 but want to move it to Linux/Alpha also.  I need an
>Alpha system.
>
>My plan is to buy a cheap system now then a faster one later.
>I have no "feel" for how fast Alphas are compared to equally
>equipped Pentiums.  For example how would a 166Mhz Alpha compare
>to a 166Mhz Pentium?  How about a couple of 233Mhz systems?
Alpha is a RISC chip, so the old "rule-of-thumb" is to divide the clockrate
by 2 to get the approximate performance level in an equivalent CISC chip.
In the case of the generation of Alpha you are mentioning (21066) they had
a few good qualtities in pipelines, cache, and so forth, so I would equate
this to be more a case of divide by 1.66. In other words it runs integer
about like a P100.
The newer Alphas are even more advanced, so that a 533MHz 21164A-2 runs
about as fast at integer as a P2 XEON 450. Of course the large advanced
multi-level cache helps a lot!


>If I know some kind of ratio of Alpha to Pentium speeds I
>could make an informed buy.  I plan to telnet into the Alpha
>and have my /home directory automounted.  All I'd run on it
>are gcc, ddd and nedit.
>
>Now if I wanted an Alpha that would blow away our Intel system
>(400Mkz PII 256Mb RAM UWSSCSI) what should I be looking at?
>I'll need something on a level with a high end ultra SPARC.
Again, the newest 21164A-2 chips are in this class. The fastest you can buy
and run right now is a 667MHz unit. It does SPEC95 int at about 23 and fp
at about 30.
In a month or 2 you can buy a 21264 and that will run at 500MHz initially,
yielding numbers around int=40 and fp=55.

A 333MHz UltraSPARC from what I recall will do about a int=16 and an fp=19



