it ain't just pine!!

> Pine 4.05 compiles with a ton of "int cast to pointer of different size"
> messages that (I think) shouldn't matter.  But it's got an even weirder
> problem:
> 
>    It only picks up characters 4 previous to what you've just typed.
> 
> Weird, weird, weird: If I start pine, and I want to immediately quit
> I have to type "q", followed by 4 other keystrokes.  The "q" gets picked
> up by pine, which exits, and the shell gets the next 3 characters.
> 
> What the hell is going on here?  Starting Pine 4.05 changed all my saved
> mail files around so I can't revert to v3.96.

I'm having the same problem (and it's probably 4 keystrokes total for you
one for the 'q' you type and then 3 other keystrokes) with some BBS software
that compiles with 0 warnings, links fine...it should work, right?...
Nope...I have the SAME problem when people try to login to the BBS.  A
programmer friend of mine and I have narrowed it down to a few
possibilities.

1) a bug in select();

2) a bug in read();

3) a bug in the alpha kernel source tree (select(); resides in the kernel
                                          source, libc just has a stub
                                          that references the kernel
                                          source) 

4) a bug in glibc2 (either a bug that was in libc5 and corrected in libc6
                    or just a bug in libc6)
5) a bug in egcs

A programmer at work and I are looking at select(); and read(); as our prime
suspects.

Our reasoning for targeting these two functions comes from the strace I did
on the bbs executable running on both my intel x86 box (running libc5 and
gcc 2.7.2.3) and my alpha (running egcs 1.1b and libc6)

On the intel box, when someone types in their bbs username, this series of
select(); and read();'s (and write();'s, one for each char typed) works
correctly... for each character typed, the read's return 1 (and the char
typed thusly:

alarm(60)                               = 0
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, {0, 0})      = 0 (Timeout)
write(1, "\r\nWelcome to The Apocalypse BB"..., 523) = 523
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, NULL)        = 1 (in [0])
read(0, "p", 256)                       = 1
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, {0, 0})      = 0 (Timeout)
write(1, "p", 1)                        = 1
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, NULL)        = 1 (in [0])
read(0, "o", 256)                       = 1
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, {0, 0})      = 0 (Timeout)
write(1, "o", 1)                        = 1
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, NULL)        = 1 (in [0])
read(0, "o", 256)                       = 1
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, {0, 0})      = 0 (Timeout)
write(1, "o", 1)                        = 1
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, NULL)        = 1 (in [0])
read(0, "h", 256)                       = 1
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, {0, 0})      = 0 (Timeout)
write(1, "h", 1)                        = 1
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, NULL)        = 1 (in [0])
read(0, "\r", 256)                      = 1
write(1, "\r\n", 2)                     = 2
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, {0, 0})      = 0 (Timeout)
write(1, "Password: ", 10)              = 10

Now obviously, the userid has been entered and the user gets 

Ok...same scenario on the alpha...except it takes 4 keystrokes for input to
be processed....like so:


setitimer(ITIMER_REAL, {it_interval={0, 0}, it_value={60, 0}}, {it_interval={0, 0}, it_value={0, 0}}) = 0
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, {0, 0})      = 0 (Timeout)
write(1, "\r\nWelcome to The Apocalypse BB"..., 523) = 523
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, NULL)        = 1 (in [0])
read(0, "pooh", 256)                    = 4
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, {0, 0})      = 0 (Timeout)
write(1, "pooh", 4)                     = 4
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, NULL)        = ? ERESTARTNOHAND (To be restarted)


Ok...now...for SOME reason, read was waiting for 4 keystrokes (and returned
4) ... but WHY????

If this rings any bells with anyone out there...PLEASE let us know  (also,
if you have pppd working (with pap authentication) email me...I'm having
trouble with that too!)



