I think Linux vendors come in 2 categories: "full service" vendors who
provide installed pre-configured systems, and "friendly" vendors who
will sell you a system without bundled Microsoft software. The
"friendly" vendors may or may not have a clue as to which hardware is
Linux compatible.

I have bought a few "full service" x86 machines from VA Research. But
I mostly roll my own using linux-friendly vendors.

I think the FAQ ought to classify vendors in these categories, and we
should accumulate complaints from customers (not relayed by the
competition) about full service vendors which don't have a clue. I
won't pay extra for full service if it's bad service.

> If a vendor isn't involved on this list or the Alpha
> newsgroup answering questions, their price would have
> to be pretty damn good to consider buying from them.

That's your choice, but I think it's wrong. A vendor might be very
competant and yet quiet on those forums. We don't have a shortage of
people to answer easy questions; we could use more people to work
behind-the scenes on the hard stuff, like getting a working aic7xxx
driver into the 2.0.X alpha kernel series.

-- g


