Hey folks!
I apologise for offending someone.
I guess what I said WAS dangerously close to "spam".
After seeing some of the recent political commercials in the U.S. in the
last month I can see why people might be sensisitive. Negative advertising
is not a good thing, and in that sense he was right.

However, as I said, I find it hard to believe that without being on the Red
Hat and Debian axp lists one could have much of a handle on Linux as used
on Alpha machines.
That aside, many of you want to do your own installation, configuration,
and implementations, and could care less if a vendor does anything other
than shipping parts.
So be it.

Onwards and upwards.

We need to address a REAL issue that is existant in Alpha-Linux:
  The lack of applications being ported.
On Intel Linux we now have the overall support of a large, and growing
groundswell. Even the Ziff-Davis PC/Mag guys are posting shareware and Open
Source listings on their mailers. Of course this is almost ALL x86 wares.

  Not to be even CLOSE to "conspiracy theory" style, I DO want to point out
the recent investment of Intel in Red Hat. While I don't belive it will
stop them from carrying on Alpha distribution, money DOES talk. Another
example is the fact that there are essentially NONE of the "commercial"
applications that RH includes for X86 on their Alpha distribution.

We need to form a co-operative team of people who will co-ordinate and
communicate efforts to promote and support Alpha Linux application porting.

This is the "opening shot" at this direction. I now invite
comments/flames/discussions from the other readers on this list. 


