On Fri, 6 Nov 1998, Thunderhawk Alpha wrote:

> >We need to address a REAL issue that is existant in Alpha-Linux:
> >  The lack of applications being ported.

Indeed. 


> This is true just about everywhere in the download software places. Even
> the 'free for non-commercial use' Linux software sites are Intel-centric.

Right. This is what I'm concerned most about. I don't mind seeing
commercial applications not being ported. I mean, I do .. but I understand
it. Companies are there to make money, and if there are not enought
Linux/Alpha people, then thats that: no software. This doesn't get under
my skin so much .. I started out with computers on obscure hardware and
learned how to program just because I needed applications.. I'm used to
this.

What concerns me is the lack of Alpha-compatible code in the public
domain area. 

Now, having just completed porting all of my code to the Alpha, I can
say that I didn't even give a thought to some of the bit fiddling that
I did, or the way I used longs.. sure, if someone mentioned that the
code wouldn't work on a 64-bit machine, I would have taken note of that.

Everyone programmer ought to know about the problem, but not everyone
realizes it, and actually is mindful of it when they are coding. 

They fact of the matter is, most Linux users are Intel guys .. and chances
are most people who put out code are Intel users. For what its worth, most
programmers that I have been in touch with about their code not working on
an Alpha were eager to help get it working. This is why people give away
code, so that others can use it .. not to support Intel's superior market
share. 

If we are going to get appz .. we need to preach on the programming and
tech lists; and maybe we ought to discuss porting issues of some of the
larger packages elsewhere (that is, NOT an alpha-list). The guys who
make the mistakes aren't here .. they are on the other side of the fence!

> Note that Corel signed an agreement to get RedHat to make a distro for
> their Netwinder (StrongArm CPU) machine.

Sweet .. I want to get one of those machines some day.

> Keep up the good work and hopefully everyone else will do the same...

Well, one of the things I am doing, and I hope others may take a page
from my book here, is to actively work to port code, just for the sake
of porting it. 

I'm going to be going through the sunsite archive's apps/circuits
directory and I'm going to port as many programs as I can. The same
goes for any spectrum analysis or other general DSP packages that
are nice. I do a bit of electrical design, so I've adopted this
cause. 

If people can adopt highways to clean up, we can adopt code. 

