Be careful making any kind of general statement in this area, as the
situation with these binaries may be similar to the Netscape OSF binary: it
is built to run in a 32-bit environment, because the source is NOT 64-bit
clean.

We've all seen or heard of the problems with the Mozilla sources, and how
unclean they are WRT to 64-bits. Don't think that taking the easy way out
when "porting" to Alpha only occurred to them... :-\ 

Now, I'd be very surprised if the Oracle sources haven't been cleansed of
any 32-bit-isms, because it makes sense for them to take advantage of the
64-bit environment (as it would for most database apps). But what is the
advantage for a browser, or a spreadsheet, or a word processor, that makes
a 64-bit clean port worth the time and effort?

