I know I'll get flamed nastily for this, but cleaning up the source makes
sense for the future, althought for now, a seemingly distant future, sums
in one word: Merced.  Yes, hiss, boo, but think about it.  Most companies
are technology conformists, they use what everyone else uses for the
desktop: the PC.  From a practical standpoint, the henpecked journalists,
IT professionals, analysts and so on believe that because Intel / Microsoft
are dominant now, they are the wave of the future, which many believe to be
either a good bet or a sure bet.  If it's such a good bet, why not clean up
now and stay clean, so that Merced would be a more straight forward port?

I do agree that sometimes we go overboard thinking in terms of 64 bit
system is so much better than a 32 bit one.  In terms most 'office' tools,
a 32 bit chip can be as good as a 64 bit one, as the program doesn't get to
that memory limit of 32 bit and the extra computational width isn't very
usable for an office app.

What are all the issues in calling a program 64-bit clean anyways?  Is it
primarily due to data type definitions or are there other problems.  I see
the term 64-bit clean written about here and there, but never truly
understanding all of what it means.  I do OK programming, so possible
programming jargon won't offend me, and I hope others would like to see a
good description.

Thanks

JRDM


