I agree that it's difficult and expensive to bring a barebones UDB up to
the point where it is a useful standalone system with an internal disk,
and as far as I'm concerned, there is no point in getting a UDB, which
is this nice, compact, elegant little box, and hanging an external SCSI
box off of it.

But I have to say that I love the two UDBs I bought a while back which I
run diskless.  A UDB with 64M of RAM and no disk boots off the network
into a great "spare" workstation/terminal running X.  I had disk space
to spare on my (non-Alpha) NFS server for holding the Alpha Linux
filesystem, and of course I would want my home directory on the NFS
server anyway.  So I have this inexpensive, compact and fairly quiet box
on a bookshelf in my living room with the monitor and keyboard where I
can reach them from the couch where I watch TV.  And another one in my
computer room as a spare machine/terminal.  And if I wanted to add a
third I would just plug it in to the Ethernet hub, add one line to the
bootptab on the server and it would boot into Linux.  It's great.

I did find a couple cheap 2.5" SCSI disks but I haven't found the cable
for connecting them to the system and they seem to be a couple
millimeters two high to fit in the system without modifying the mounting
bay and/or removing the floppy drive.  I haven't bothered to try.

I wouldn't be able to do development on it and keep my sanity (it takes
several hours to compile the kernel as I vaguely recall from the one
time I tried it).  And it's a bummer that there isn't a Java-enabled
browser that runs locally on it.  It would be completely useless if it
wasn't hanging off of a "real" server.  But it's definitely worth the
$300 I paid for it, and now you can get them for $99.  What a deal!

