To: axp-list@redhat.com
In-Reply-To: <3.0.5.32.19981107103737.009a65c0@pop.webcom.com>
Message-ID: <ML-3.3.910560344.9124.davidm@fuzzbox.fuzzbox.ml.org>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: TEXT/PLAIN; CHARSET=US-ASCII

My experience with a ruffian suggests that you do NOT need to use arcinst.exe;
the standard Linux tools will do just fine. I've also heard that arcinst and
fdisk don't always agree on the resulting partition table when it is used, but
I can't vouch for that.

The main point is that ARCS BIOS (which is what the ruffian uses, and is
different to ARC and AlphaBIOS and SRM and anything else on the planet) appears
to only understand 16 bit FAT partitions on hard drives, and doesn't seem to
like VFAT-style directories either.

So the following works for me:

Create the partition with type 4 (DOS 16-bit <32M)
Use mkdosfs -F 16 to create the filesystem
Then mount it with -tmsdos (to make sure that you don't create vfat entries),
and copy ldmilo.exe and milo onto the partition.

The important variables are probably these (assuming sda1):
OSLoadPartition = scsi(0)disk(0)rdisk(0)partition(1)
OSLoader = scsi(0)disk(0)rdisk(0)partition(1)\ldmilo.exe

Oh, and you should be able to use the ARCS BIOS directory list command (Enter
Setup, the I think it is System Configuration; at this point you should be able
to wander around the device tree; hit Enter on the node corresponding to
scsi(0)disk(0)rdisk(0), and you should be able to get a directory listing of
partition 1 (which should show ldmilo.exe and milo of course).

Other ruffian gotchas:
At least some versions of MILO only work if the video card is in the 64bit PCI
slot (the long one), even if it is a 32 bit card. The reason for this is that
the other slots are actually behind a PCI-PCI bridge, and MILO seems to look
only on the primary PCI bus.

There may be a problem accessing memory above 224M (if you have that much); the
current hypothesis is that the physical memory is not mapped contiguously and
has a hole at this point. The only current workaround is to do a

set MEMORY_SIZE 224

at the milo prompt before booting. This unfortunately makes it impossible to
have the machine auto boot; it should be possible using a command like
set MEMORY_SIZE 224; boot ...

but the stupid ARCS BIOS mangles strings that are put into the OSLoadOptions
variable in an environment; firstly it appears to lower-case them (and MILO
doesn't treat memory_size the same as MEMORY_SIZE) and secondly it treats the
';' as and end of line character and throws the rest away. Workarounds should
appear in newer versions of milo (and may have already for all I know).



