You wouldn't happen to be using Red Hat Linux, would you?  I seem to
remember also that Red Hat ships the correct version of automake, but
it's broken as compiled.  This is where I fixed the problem on a Red
Hat Linux machine at work.  In this case, you will need to remove your
original automake via rpm (rpm -e automake-x.x) or else the automake in
/usr/bin will pre-empt the correctly installed version in /usr/local/bin.

If you did something to defeat this logic (aka: correctly upgraded the
package in /usr/bin), ignore my advice.  This is how I fixed the problem
at work, and I'm still not sure why the Red Hat-installed version
doesn't work.

-- 
Shaw Terwilliger (twig@advancenet.net)


