Stefan Boresch wrote:
> 
> Sorry for this follow-up:
> 
> > Are there any (utility) programs around that allow one to see what the
> > AlphaBIOS (5.62-1a,5.66-1 on an LX) actually stores in its NVRAM.
> > Removing the battery just resets the clock.
> 
> By looking more closely, and comparing to an Intel machine, I have the
> following piece of add. information.  Apparently, the SCSI controller
> (AHA 2940U, and yes, it worked for 4 months without problems) gets IRQ
> 8 (I/O Port Address) assigned on the malfunctioning LX.  This is what
> the SCSI BIOS after hitting Ctrl-A reports.  This looks very fishy to
> me (my LX manual says that IRQ 8 is reserved...?)
> 
> Any suggestions (or do I have to assume that my PCI system is bad...?)
> 
> Stefan Boresch
> 

I've found Adaptec PCI cards do not recognize when a
PCI interrupt has been used and will allocate one that
a previous card has already taken. The fix for me, has
been to always place the Adaptec 1st in the PCI bus so
it has no competition. "It's a feature, not a bug...".

Doesn't matter whether it is Intel or Alpha as I've
seen it on both.

Of course if your kernel has interrupt sharing enabled,
it should still run. Just wouldn't want two high interrupt
devices competing for the same interrupt. A network
card and SCSI card come to mind as ones not to put on
the same interrupt, particularly if you do lots of NFS
from that system.

YMMV


Wes


