Not to flame a fight between Intel/AXP, however, these are the results
from some tests I've made with the _REAL_ program I mainly use.

It is a fortran program, highly vectorized. What I need, is something to
crunch numbers fast. Quite good I/O, roughly 300 MB of scratch files 
on the fly. That's all. On the AXP, I use a DEC compiled exectuable.

So...

I have timings for a P-Pro 200, a P-II 233, a real DEC 433 and an AXP 600.

Assuming that for each platfrom the timings are linearly scaling with the
clock (it works between the P-Pro and the P-II 233 as well as between the
DEC 433 and the AXP 600) I would have the following scaling:

   CPU     Speed
PPro 200    1.00
PII  233    1.12
PII  450    2.16
DEC  433    2.81
AXP  533    3.47
AXP  600    3.91

The AXP 533 / PII 450 ratio would be roughly 1.6

According to my test (I remark not based on any SPEC benchmarks, but with
my _real_ program) not only the DEC 533 is defenitely faster than the PII
450, but even the DEC 433 is faster...  Maybe, the fact that the the AXP
are 64 bit, and the fact that the executable is DEC compiled, helps,
I don't really know. I'm not a guru, and thus I've to stick to simple
facts I can test.

Also, I agree with Thorsten that the PII 450 wouldn't be cheaper...

Since I haven't bought the computer yet, any further suggestions
appreciated.

Luigi

