> The expansion ROM cannot assign IRQs, BARS, or any other resource
> mapping. That would mess up the BIOS's attempt to arrange things.
> 
> Besides, the expansion rom is optional.

wsb@paralleldata.com said:
> It's optional on 2940/2940UW/3940W?? Don't think so. 

No, I meant a PCI design is not required to provide one. However, a specific
design *is* allowed to require the EROM be run to make it whole. This is
fairly common with video cards and devices that support being boot from.

wsb@paralleldata.com said:
> Guaranteed the 3940W uses multiple IRQ's. I see it when Win3.1 starts
> up in my other partition.

I believe it. The standard allows for it. That is what the "interrupt pin"
register is for. A device can also have multiple function units.

  Bus  2, device   5, function  0:
    SCSI storage controller: Adaptec AIC-7872 (rev 3).
      Medium devsel.  Fast back-to-back capable.  IRQ 11.  Master Capable.  Latency=64.  Min
Gnt=8.Max Lat=8.
      I/O at 0xd400.
      Non-prefetchable 32 bit memory at 0xfdafe000.
  Bus  2, device   4, function  0:
    SCSI storage controller: Adaptec AIC-7872 (rev 3).
      Medium devsel.  Fast back-to-back capable.  IRQ 9.  Master Capable.  Latency=64.  Min
Gnt=8.Max Lat=8.
      I/O at 0xd800.
      Non-prefetchable 32 bit memory at 0xfdaff000.


I would love to see the output of "lspci -vx" on your system. There is
something highly funky here. A single card is not supposed to have more
than 1 device number. (It can have 8 *function* units, but only 1 device.)

Are you sure there is not another AIC7872 on your motherboard?
