>      Non-prefetchable 32 bit memory at 0xfd90fd90.
>      Non-prefetchable 32 bit memory at 0xf460f460.

Those BAR values look bogus to me, as a matter of passing interest.

>Then it makes me wonder how IRQ's are handled across
>PCI-PCI bridges. I know this card is a pain in the butt
>to get working because it's use of IRQ's conflicts with
>the slots on the motherboard. That's part of what I was
>saying before. I assume when a bridge is used, you have
>no choice except to share IRQ's since the PCI bus has
>only 4 IRQ's??

How IRQs are handled in a PCI system is left up to the discretion of the 
board designer to quite a large extent.  Each PCI *slot* has four IRQ pins 
(INTA..INTD) but the bus itself doesn't enforce any IRQ policy.  Usually the 
way things work is that there are four IRQ lines on the bus itself and the INT 
lines from each slot attach to this in a rotating arrangement (which means 
that with up to four devices each using only one interrupt, each one will get 
a different line).  But it would be equally valid for all four INT pins from 
every slot to be just connected to a single interrupt line, or for every INT 
pin to have its own input into the system IRQ controller, or any other 
combination you could think of.

When a PCI/PCI bridge is involved, especially on an add-in card, yes it 
usually combines however many IRQ inputs it accepts from the devices behind it 
and just presents a single interrupt request to the primary bus.  But if the 
bridge was integrated directly onto the motherboard this might not be the case 
- the interrupts could just go round the side of the bridge and straight into 
the IRQ controller.

p.



