I've done something similar, but didn't use diald.  This will not fix your
problem with diald, but the workaround is so simple that it may be worth
dropping diald...

If you have your kernel compiled with loadable modules, if the kernel tries
to route a packet that it has no route to, it will look for the script
/sbin/request-route.  If that script exists, it will execute it.  Your ppp
connection script can be called from there.

I spent several days trying to get diald to work on my system.  Heard about
this and had it working literally in 10 minutes (granted, on an Intel box).
Only disadvantage I can think of is that it is Linux-specific.  This is/was
documented in a howto that I can't currently locate on sunsite, but I
believe it's on my 5.1 CD.  I thought the name was like home server
mini-howto or some such thing-- discussed setting up a small box as a
dial-on-demand server, plus using smb to share disk and print services.

Of course, all standard disclaimers apply...

