Quoting Ronald Bruck (bruck@math.usc.edu):
> >Stephen Williams wrote:
> >
> >Alphas and Intels are little endian.
> >
> 
> Actually, I thought the Alpha could go little-endian OR big-endian.

I don't think so, but I haven't seen any direct evidence either way.  I know
the MIPS chips (which DEC used for a couple of DECStations before the Alpha
came out) are either endian - and the MIPS C compiler had a flag to generate
either type of code - but even on the DECStations that used MIPS they used the
same endianness as Intel and Vax.

Since the Alpha was made specifically for (Open)VMS, DEC Unix and NT, all of
which were originally on little endian machines, I don't see why they would
have made it switchable.  The MIPS chips, on the other hand, were designed to
be used in a lot of different architectures, from SGIs to DECs to Nintendo, so
making it switchable makes more sense.

