>>> Stefan Boresch said:
> 
> Regardless of the slot, the Adaptec (from it's BIOS) claims to sit on
> IRQ 8 (for what this is worth).

As mentioned before, what gets put into the "Interrupt Line" register
is used only by system software; the card doesn't care.

On most Alphas, we have both the normal 16 ISA PIC interrupts, as well as
a number of additional PCI interrupts (number dependent on the platform).
For a PCI card, the "Interrupt Line" probably indicates a PCI interrupt
rather than an ISA one, so there should be no conflict with other devices.

Note that for almost all ALpha platforms, the values set by the consoles
(either ARC, AlphaBIOS, or SRM) are different from the values that MILO or
the LINUX kernel set, simply because the numbering schemes of the system
software are different.

--Jay++

