> > 
> > In the file au.c:
> > 
> > #define SUN_MAGIC       0x2e736e64              /* Really '.snd' */
> > #define SUN_INV_MAGIC   0x646e732e              /* '.snd' upside-down */
> > .
> > .
> > #define SUN_HDRSIZE     24                      /* Size of minimal header *
>/
> > .
> > #define SUN_ULAW        1                       /* u-law encoding */
> > 
> > If SUN_INV_MAGIC is found then swap is set to 1. The header of a .au file
> > is:
> > 
> >      .snd^@^@^@(^@^@dT^@^@^@^A
> > 
> > where
> > 
> >      .snd = SUN_MAGIC
> >       ^@^@^@( = SUN_HDRSIZE
> >      ^@^@dT = data size
> >      ^@^@^@^A = SUN_ULAW (encoding)
> > 
> > I think I just saw a mistake, the SUN_HDRSIZE and SUN_ULAW should be 24L
> > and 1L? Anyway the error is reported on the encoding.
> > 
> > The header seems to be big endian.
> > 
> 
> OK now I see how there might be some confusion.
> Whether a particular file format is big endian or
> little endian is up to the creator of the format.
> 
> This does not mean the machine you use it on is
> the same endianess. Given the specification of
> the file format, one can easily convert between
> endianess and do whatever one likes with the file.
> However, no matter what endian system you have, you
> must have code in the program to take care of
> that problem. I do the same thing in my own code.
> In much of the file formats used in the petroleum
> industry, big endian wins out because when the
> format was created, the creator happened to be on
> a big endian system. So, whenever I have to read
> those formats on Intel/Alpha, I have to swap bytes
> around to make any sense of the data.
> 
> So you need to worry about both the endianess of
> the machine and the endianess of the file and have
> code to make them match up or you get nonsense.

Quite true. However, sox looks at the MAGIC 4 bytes as a long, compares
that with the #define and determins endianess from that. It appears that
the correct endianess is detected (at least the error message is not
printed). Where I get a failure is the encoding code. The SUN_ULAW is
not detected and the case falls through to the default case printing
the error message.

I read an email on this list where someone is running sox fine on their
Red Hat 5.1 Alpha. I just rebuilt sox from the sox src rpm from my 5.1 CD
and still get the same error.

It is not worth my time to continue with this. I will try the sox with
5.2.

Thanks everyone for your comments!

