>is more cost effective.  For the compute server application
>we don't need fancy graphics cards or monitor.  (Remember
>that we are talking about the gcc compiler so it is not
>fair to quote spec numbers derived from the DEC compiler.)
>
This is a fundamentally misleading path you have started on Chris.
Why? Because specmarks are far from the question you need answers to.

You MIGHT get great results on an Alpha/Linux machine, and it also may,
<ahem> "suck".
There is a beter chance with DEC libraries for many problems, but then
again maybe not.
For example, if you were to choose LAPACK/LINPACK bench numbers it can be
shown that with stock libraries the Alpha on Linux would be horrible. BUT,
if you use some of the recent library optimisations from Goto ands friends,
there is a more than even chance that Linux would absolutely "blow away"
the DEC compiler/libraries.

The ONLY real way you can answer  your questtion is to get the code for the
problem, and some real-world sample data, have a few people look at it from
an optimisation and "64-bit-cleanness" point of view and try it on the
repective machines, and compare the results.

Otherwise you will be at best fooling yourself, and at worst, wasting your
money.

Benchmarks and damned lies is NOT a way to buy computers.


