First, much thanks to all who responded to my question. My main point is that
the code *does* run on an Intel machine but not on (at least my) Alpha, so this
list seemed an appropriate place to ask.

 
> 
> You know, this may be completely off here, but I'm wondering if the "&&"
> is somehow not correct in ANDing the result of (wd->m_id == id) on a
> platform where the size of "wd" (pointer) is not the same size as 
> "(wd->m_id == id)" (bool same as int which is != size of pointer?).  Wild
> guess here.  :)  I would assume this possibility is very remote 
> since the evaluation is essentially "if(wd){if(wd->m_id==id) {} }", but
> I haven't looked at the compiler source; it may be optimizing something
> funny.


Most problems where code runs on an Intel and not on an Alpha seem to be
related to the pointer/integer size difference so what you say may make sense.

I'm considering dropping back from egcs 1.1b to 1.0.3a to see what happens. As
you know the code is already being compiled with no optimizations. 

Heinz Ulrich Stille asked whether callback_action was 32 or 64 bit, I'm not
enough of a programmer to know the answer to this.

The lack of a decent WP for the alpha platform has been a real bugaboo for me,
so I'd really like to see this problem resolved, any additional alpha-guru
suggestions are always appreciated.

