It's not a bug. People seem to think every point they don't understand is
a MM bug.

From http://www.uwsg.indiana.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/9805.0/0445.html

> While under heavy load and with almost no free mem, my kernel spits
> out some kernel: Unable to load interpreter I located that printk() in
> fs/binfmt_elf.c at line 632, but couldn't find out WHY it would get
> issued. Is it bad or just a warning, and what interpreter? Can someone
> enlighten me? :)

Dynamically linked ELF executables contain an "interpreter" field
which typically names the dynamic loader (e.g. /lib/ld-linux.so.1 or
/lib/ld-linux.so.2). This message means that your system has so
little free memory it can't even load ld-linux.so, which makes it a
bit difficult to run anything. :-)

